 ],
 "literal": "Like the curved flower of the summer trumpet-flower tree / with beautiful hair flowing, and dark, abundant beauty, / as if you brought that young woman wearing fine ornaments, / you wake me from my sweet sleep, O dream! / They will not mock, indeed, those who have separated from their partners.",
 "translation": "O dream! You wake me from my sweet sleep, appearing as if you have brought back my young woman adorned with fine jewels, whose dark beauty is like the curved flower of the summer trumpet-flower tree and whose beautiful hair flows. Those who have separated from their beloved partners will surely not mock you for this.",
 "modern": "கோடைகாலத்துப் பாதிரி மரத்தின் வளைந்த மலரைப் போன்ற, அழகிய கூந்தல் ஒழுகும், கரிய அழகைக் கொண்ட, நுட்பமான அணிகலன்களை அணிந்த என் தலைவியைக் கொண்டுவந்து தருவது போல, என் இனிய தூக்கத்தைக் கலைக்கிறாய், கனவே! தம் துணையைப் பிரிந்து வாழ்பவர்கள் உன்னை இகழ மாட்டார்கள்.",
 "explanation": "The hero is traveling through the arid desert path (pālai) on a mission, separated from his beloved. During his journey, he falls into a sweet sleep and dreams of his beloved, whose dark beauty and flowing hair resemble the curved, beautiful trumpet-flower of the summer. The dream is so vivid that it feels as if the dream itself has brought her to him, but then he suddenly wakes up to find himself alone. Instead of being angry at the dream for waking him and shattering the illusion, he feels grateful for the brief, sweet reunion. He addresses the dream, saying that those who suffer the pain of separation from their partners will never mock or blame the dream, because even a fleeting moment of her presence in a dream is a precious comfort.",
